 "notes": "NASDUCK Floquet (Bloch et al., Sci. Adv. 8, eabl8919; arXiv:2105.04603). Curve is the bright-blue 'Mean' line (binned log-space average of the dense 95% C.L. bound band) of Fig. 2, the paper's headline ALP-neutron limit over 4e-15 to 4e-12 eV. Extracted programmatically from the vector figure rendered at 400 dpi: axes calibrated from vector tick-mark coordinates (x major ticks at 1e-14/1e-13/1e-12 eV, y decade spacing 10.778 pt), per-column median of the dark-blue 1-sigma band pixels (whose center is the mean line), median-filtered and binned to ~100 log-spaced masses; overlay-verified. Paper reports g_aNN in GeV^-1 defined via H = gamma_Xe b_DM cos(...) . I_Xe with b_DM = eps_N g_aNN sqrt(2 rho_DM hbar c^3) v / gamma_Xe (Eqs. 1-2), i.e. a gradient coupling in GeV^-1; converted to canonical dimensionless g_an with the compilation's 2m_N rule: g_an = 2 x 0.93957 GeV x g_aNN. Caveat: since the paper's Hamiltonian couples to the spin operator I (=sigma/2), a literal matching would give g_an = m_n g_aNN, a factor 2 below; the 2m_N rule was applied as instructed. Assumes eps_N = 0.63 (NNLOGO(394) nuclear model) and rho_DM = 0.4 GeV/cm^3. Proton-coupling and quadratic-coupling bounds (Fig. 3) also reported by the paper are not included. The paper's precise tabulated bounds are on Zenodo (record 5598215), not used here (outside allowed inputs); the actual per-frequency bounds fluctuate around the reported mean by up to the 'worst/best' band (roughly factor ~2-3).",
